WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.250
Change analysis compares observations across time.

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:10.401
Before-and-after imagery can support construction tracking, damage assessment, environmental review, route status, and event timelines.

00:00:10.401 --> 00:00:17.552
The images must be comparable enough that collection differences are not mistaken for scene changes.

00:00:17.552 --> 00:00:26.003
Repeat flights or observations should use consistent location, angle, altitude, lens, orientation, and coverage where safe and lawful.

00:00:26.003 --> 00:00:33.154
Record deviations because a shifted viewpoint can reveal or hide features and alter apparent size.

00:00:33.154 --> 00:00:35.104
Seasonal differences matter.

00:00:35.104 --> 00:00:41.388
Leaf cover, snow, water level, crop state, and ground color may change normally.

00:00:41.388 --> 00:00:45.939
Lighting and shadow direction vary with time and weather.

00:00:45.939 --> 00:00:50.490
These effects can resemble damage, removal, or new objects.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:55.473
For construction or damage assessment, define measurable features before comparison.

00:00:55.473 --> 00:01:00.891
Record visible additions, removals, displacement, surface change, access change, or debris.

00:01:00.891 --> 00:01:05.875
Avoid assigning cause or severity beyond what the imagery supports.

00:01:05.875 --> 00:01:07.825
Preserve event chronology.

00:01:07.825 --> 00:01:13.676
Distinguish capture time, file time, upload time, processing time, and report time.

00:01:13.676 --> 00:01:22.560
Metadata can help but may be missing or incorrect, so corroborate important timing with flight logs and other records.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:31.445
A useful comparison explains what changed, what remained stable, how collection conditions differed, and how confident the judgment is.

00:01:31.445 --> 00:01:36.862
It also states what ground inspection or independent evidence is needed.
